A||N||25||Seanh||Removing the Stupid Head's (V6)(Long)||||8||
Z||000000||Seanh||10-16-2000||10:57 PM||||Well, I started following the Haynes manual's procedure for remove the heads. I am at the point where I am to remove the intake manifold, what fun. In the sub-step of removing the manifold, there is another sub-step for remove the rocker arm covers. I am at step 3 for removing the Front rocker arm covers, (I understand it to be easier to remove the front first, then the back) I have no Idea: It says to <I><B>"Remove the Vacuum boost line and tube".</I></B> Thats it. What is this and were is it and how do I remove it?  <P>Also on removing the rear cover, I am having trouble with the "torque reaction rod" I am assuming they mean the dogbone, am I right? It says to <I><B>"Disconnect the torque reaction rod bolt at the cylinder head bracket".</B></I> I think I know what they are talking about, but, Should I support the engine before I remove it? It looks to me like it is under stress and wont come out without rolling the engine back a little,<B>{Remember, I am not sure about that one, thats just what it looks like).</B><BR>Next it says to <B><I>"Swing the torque reaction rod up and remove the the bolt connecting the cylinder head bracket to the bracket at the front of the engine.</I></B> Also, I am pretty sure I know what they are telling me to do there also. But then it hits me with this: <B><I>Loosen the lower bolt of the torque reaction rod bracket at the front of the engine.</I></B> Not a clue what that means. Is it the bracket on the rear headers, or the one on the front headers. I think it is the front, but I am having trouble seeing the bracket up there. I remove the thermo housing and hoses to get a better view, but still cant see it very good. Also not Idea what these following two mean either, (perhaps I will understand them once I get the previous one figured out) <I><B>Remove the upper two bolts of the torque reaction rod bracket at the front of the engine.</I></B> And <I><B>Remove the torque reaction rod bracket bolt at the cylinder head/exhaust manifold connection.</I></B> Once again, not a clue on that, but will probally be able to understand it if I figure out the previous ones.<P>Other then those minor problems, I am looking good, hopefully I will be able to do some other things to the engine that need to be done like replacing the plastic vacuum hoses under the upper intake plenum, and bigger fuel injectors.<P>I was wondering, what fuel injectors should I use on the stock setup, with a few modifications? I probally wont do it if I have to get the ones from the Fiero Store. But any other bigger ones off other cars I might be able to get cheap? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System<p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 10-16-2000).]||||reg||8
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||10-16-2000||11:15 PM||||OK, I'm looking trying to sort things out for you.<BR>1) The vac. boost tube goes to the power brake booster up front. It should be 3/4 to 1 inch in diameter.<BR>2) Correct assumption.<BR>3&4) Remove bracket, (torque reaction rod) part of it mounts on the front (water pump side) of the engine cylinder head.<P>In essence: take everything off that's in your way (remember where whatever goes and what attaches it!).<BR>You may find it easier to lay things out in order of removeal with it's hardware for ease of reassembly!<P>Hope this helps!<P>IRG||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||10-16-2000||11:32 PM||||Thanks<P>That clears it up more. I am still wondering about removing the dogbone, how to support the engine or if I even need to. I will try taking it out tomarrow, but it looks like it is under stress if you know what I mean.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||10-16-2000||11:32 PM||||Since all I ever do is point out obvious things, here I go again...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>I suggest you take a few photos of the work in progress, for several reasons.  First, it will help you reassemble things the way you found them - even if you forget how you found them.  Second, if you sell the car years from now, you can provide partial evidence of work done to the car while you had it.  Third, you can post pics to help people out here on the Forum.  Fourth, you can keep the photos as a lasting memory of this happy adventure.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||10-16-2000||11:45 PM||||GOOD IDEA FROM Stugood!<BR>You should not have to 'support' the engine, it may rotate forward abit when you remove the dogbone. When you get to the front head just rotate to the rear and tie it back with something. Makes it easier to get to.||||reg||1
