Z||000010||BBBAD GT||10-18-2000||09:20 AM||||Well, I don't want to drag this out...but here is the thread <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/004704.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/004704.html</A>  where you say:<P>"I have never used them. But if your looking for a good V8 kit, check out     <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com" TARGET=_blank>www.v8archie.com</A>   One of my friend purchased a kit from him to put in his 87 GT, and I help install it, and have driven it many times. Were both very happy with its preformance. Although the shipping was a little slow. Its a great kit."<P>What gives?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Carrolles||10-18-2000||12:01 PM||||Let's all settle down.  Seanh asked for advice.  Isn't the purpose of this forum to share information?  We all learn by asking questions.<P>You shouldn't have to support the engine when you remove the torque reaction rod (dogbone aka strut).  The bracket that supports the dogbone is mounted with 5 bolts.  One bolt is vertical and is attached near the center rear spark plug.  Three are on the right side of the rear head facing to the right.  I believe the lowest one is bolted through the front engine mount.  It can be removed if needed, just don't remove both engine mount bolts on the front of the engine or it will drop and will be a royal pain to line up the bolt holes to re-attach.<P>The last bolt is attached to a bracket that is bolted to the front head.  You can see the bolt at the front of the dogbone.  You'll have to remove the front dogbone bolt and swing the dogbone out of the way to access this bolt.  This bracket is bolted to the right side of the front head with two bolts and a stud (the stud is where the oil pressure sending unit is supported).  The upper A/C bracket is captured by these bolts also.  Once these bolts are removed this bracket can be snaked out of the way.  It might help to remove the battery and it's heat shield to get better access to these bolts.<P>It helps to remove the EGR solenoid assembly to give better access to the dogbone brackets.<P>Remember which bolts goes where because there are several different length bolts with the same diameter/thread pitch.  Also remember where the stud goes on the front head since the oil pressure sender mounts there.<P>Also make a note of the routing of the wiring harness and vacuum lines so you can put them back where they came from.<P>The Helms manual and 22-P manual have good diagrams of each of these brackets and how they are attached.  If you need I could scan them and post them or e-mail them to you when I am at home.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000012||prof bobo||10-18-2000||02:00 PM||||Pictures?  Who _doesn't_ own a camcorder these days?  Instant turnaround, no $ to develop the film, take all the shots at all the angles you want, leaving yourself audio hints/tips/reminders as you go.  I did this on my first rebuild, it's still kinda cool to watch the kind of time-lapse progression from grungy-to-pieces-to-clean-and-running.  If I'd edit out the cussing I could probably sell it . . .||||reg||1
Z||000013||Seanh||10-18-2000||05:15 PM||||BBBAD GT: I emailed you so this wont get off the topic.<P>I am going to get me a P-22 manual soon, but I havent sent him the money yet. That would be great if you could scan and email them to me.  <A HREF="mailto:sean@kdsi">sean@kdsi</A>  is the address. I am going to work on it again tonight. For now I will have to settle with still picts. I have 2 video cams. but one doesnt work all together and is older then I am, and the newer one works but it shows no pict on the view finder nor will it record at all. I may take on this task after the car is running again.<P>These new forum colors cool.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||6
Z||000014||Seanh||10-18-2000||09:21 PM||||I just started working on the car, and have removed the batt to check it out. How do I remove the EGR solenoid assembly?  I was reading the Haynes manual and it is saying to remove the rocker arm covers before removing the intake Plenum? Sound correct? I would think by looking at it, it would be better to remove the upper and middle intake plenum before removing the rocker arm covers? Should I do that? Also, how should I disconnect the throttle and cruise control cables to the throttle body? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||5
Z||000015||ThatRickGuy||10-18-2000||09:41 PM||||I don't think you can even get the valve covers off w/o removing the upper and middle intake. there is barily any clearance in there.||||reg||1
Z||000016||BOILERMAKER||10-18-2000||10:27 PM||||Seanh, all the initial disassembly is common sense.  In order to take part B off, part A has to come off due to physically being in the way.  If you have to take the valve covers off (which you do) you will have to take the upper and middle plenum off, that's just the nature of the beast.  Just chalk it up to a typo in the book or something.<P>Same thing with the EGR valve and throttle body (if you choose to the throttle body off seperately from the upper plenum, I don't remember off hand what I did when I replaced valve cover gaskets).  Look for obvious things that inhibit you from taking off the item you are after.  Never (unless specifically told to) use excessive force to take items off of an engine.<P>I'm not implying that you don't have common sense, but in the end that's all it really takes.<P>Good Luck, I'll be in your shoes in a month or two.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Seanh||10-18-2000||11:01 PM||||I wasnt really thinking very good the first day I started doing this. I just followed the hayne manual and it said I have to remove the rocker arm covers before I can remove the intake plenum. I thought it was odd, but I figured they knew what they are talking about, so I followed the procedure and didnt get to far as you can see. I will work a little tomarrow morning and later tomarrow night. Thanks.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000018||JimIndyRCR||10-18-2000||11:05 PM||||Sounds like you're in for a 'big' job. What I found makes this job much easier is to remove the rear most 'cradle mount' bolts and let the rear of the cradle down 'a little'. Let the rear down just enough to gain much more access to the 'front' (left side) of the engine. Hope this helps.<BR>Jim||||reg||1
Z||000019||Seanh||10-19-2000||10:03 PM||||Just thought I would update. Today I removed the throttle body, upper Intake plenum, and vacuum lines. I am currently soaking the throttle body in cleaner and was thinking. Could I get it chromed? Would it last? And another thing to add, I can see why the car run bad in the mornings, not a clogged cat, but the intake is filthy. Any good suggestions for cleaning the intake manifold? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000020||Carrolles||10-19-2000||11:36 PM||||Here are the diagrams.  I hope they come out ok.<P><BR>    <BR>Looks like it worked.<P>Take care,<P>Carroll<P>Looking at the pictures I see now that I screwed up the description of the torque strut mounting bracket.  it does not have 5 bolts.  I was thinking about the alternator bracket which is bolted to the head and the block along with the lower engine mount.  The torque strut bracket is bolted to the front of the block, the rear head near the center spark plug, and to the other bracket.<P>Anyway look at the diagrams to see what I'm having trouble describing.<P>Sorry for the confusion.<P>Carroll<P>No problem.  They are gone.<P><BR>[This message has been edited by Carrolles (edited 10-19-2000).]<P>[This message has been edited by Carrolles (edited 10-19-2000).]<P>[This message has been edited by Carrolles (edited 10-19-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by Carrolles (edited 10-20-2000).]||||reg||1
